---
title: How Persistence Works (Advanced)
id: internals
description: "The two durability layers and how they differ, thread and run identity, who owns history when both sides persist, what a reload restores, and the middleware lifecycle behind every backend."
keywords:
  - persistence internals
  - delivery durability vs state persistence
  - threadId runId
  - server authoritative
  - middleware lifecycle
---
NIFT_TANSTACK_DOCUMENT_V1
{"document":{"type":"root","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Read this when something surprised you, or before you write a backend. To simply set\npersistence up, "},{"type":"link","href":"./overview","children":[{"type":"text","value":"the overview"}]},{"type":"text","value":" is three snippets."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"two-layers-not-one","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Two layers, not one"}]},{"type":"table","align":[null,null,null,null],"header":[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Layer"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Answers"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Lives"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Docs"}]}],"rows":[[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Delivery durability"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"\"how do I reconnect to a stream that is still running?\""}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"a per-run log, keyed by "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"runId"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"link","href":"../resumable-streams/overview","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Resumable Streams"}]}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"State persistence"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"\"what is the conversation, and is it still there later?\""}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"a durable store (client and/or server)"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"this section"}]}]]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"They share no code. Delivery durability replays a live byte stream so a dropped\nconnection resumes exactly where it stopped. State persistence stores the\nconversation itself, so it survives a reload or exists on another device. A replayable\nstream is not a saved conversation, and a saved conversation is not a live stream.\nMost real apps want both."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"identity-threads-and-runs","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Identity: threads and runs"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"thread"}]},{"type":"text","value":" ("},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"threadId"},{"type":"text","value":") is the conversation: stable, survives reloads, exists on\nevery device. A "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"run"}]},{"type":"text","value":" ("},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"runId"},{"type":"text","value":") is one execution inside it, minted fresh for each\nstreamed answer. A thread accumulates many runs; delivery durability logs one run,\nstate persistence stores the whole thread."}]},{"type":"code","value":"flowchart TB\n    subgraph thread [\"One thread, threadId (stable, the conversation)\"]\n        direction LR\n        run1[\"run r1\ncompleted\"] --> run2[\"run r2\ncompleted\"] --> run3[\"run r3\nrunning\"]\n    end\n\n    subgraph delivery [\"Delivery durability, one byte log per run\"]\n        log[\"log for r3\nreplays the live stream to a reconnecting client\"]\n    end\n\n    subgraph state [\"State persistence, durable store per thread\"]\n        store[\"transcript, run records, interrupts\"]\n    end\n\n    run3 -. \"a dropped connection tails\" .-> log\n    thread -- \"saved on finish, loaded on mount\" --> store","lang":"mermaid"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Run ids are too ephemeral to reconnect by, since a reloading client may not know the\ncurrent one. Reconnection resolves from the stable "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"threadId"},{"type":"text","value":" instead: the store\nanswers \"does this thread have a live run?\" ("},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"findActiveRun"},{"type":"text","value":"), and only then does the\nclient tail that run's log. "},{"type":"link","href":"./id-map","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Id map"}]},{"type":"text","value":" is the short version of both ids."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"isolation-is-yours-to-enforce","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Isolation is yours to enforce"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Store APIs take a bare "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"threadId"},{"type":"text","value":" string, which keeps adapters simple and makes\nmulti-user isolation your job:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Derive "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"Scope.userId"},{"type":"text","value":" / "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"Scope.tenantId"},{"type":"text","value":" "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"server-side"}]},{"type":"text","value":" from session state."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Authorize before "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"loadThread"},{"type":"text","value":" / "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"saveThread"},{"type":"text","value":" / "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"reconstructChat"},{"type":"text","value":", with\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"reconstructChat({ authorize })"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Never treat a client-supplied thread id as ownership. Thread ids are guessable."}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"Scope"},{"type":"text","value":" is re-exported from "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"@tanstack/ai-persistence"},{"type":"text","value":", so the identity type sits next\nto the store contracts."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"who-owns-history-when-both-sides-persist","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Who owns history when both sides persist"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"withPersistence"},{"type":"text","value":" merges incoming "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"messages"},{"type":"text","value":" into the stored thread by id:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Empty "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"messages"}]},{"type":"text","value":": continue from the stored transcript. The server loads\nits copy and runs from there."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Non-empty "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"messages"}]},{"type":"text","value":": merge by id. The last incoming id that already\nexists in stored is a cutoff. Stored messages after it are dropped. Incoming\nwins on the same id. New ids are appended."}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"saveThread"},{"type":"text","value":" replaces the stored thread with that merged list."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Two postures:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Client-authoritative"}]},{"type":"text","value":", closest to a pure SPA. The client sends its\nin-memory list. Merge keeps stored extras the client omitted, unless a\nshared id cuts the stored tail (reload)."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Server-authoritative"}]},{"type":"text","value":": the same thread opens on another device. With\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"history: { pageSize }"},{"type":"text","value":", the client posts only the new turn. Reload posts\nthe last user. Resume posts from that user through the painted assistant.\nMerge keeps stored extras that sit before the cutoff."}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"what-a-reload-restores","children":[{"type":"text","value":"What a reload restores"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"With a client store adapter, "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"useChat"},{"type":"text","value":" reads the client record on load and acts on\nwhat it finds:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":true,"start":1,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The run had finished."}]},{"type":"text","value":" The record has the transcript and no resume pointer. The\nconversation paints instantly from storage, with no network."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The run was paused on an interrupt."}]},{"type":"text","value":" The resume pointer carries the pending\ninterrupts, so the transcript paints and the approval UI comes back as it was."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The run was still streaming."}]},{"type":"text","value":" The transcript paints from storage, then the\nclient rejoins the live run through the durability log and the reply finishes in\nplace. This is the one case that needs both layers."}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A dropped connection while the page is still open is simpler: delivery durability\nreconnects on its own. Persistence matters once the page itself is gone."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Server-authoritative mode paints from a server read instead of "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"localStorage"},{"type":"text","value":".\nA GET with "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"limit"},{"type":"text","value":" returns the newest window and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"page"},{"type":"text","value":". An older window uses\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"before"},{"type":"text","value":". The delivery log cannot supply that history, because it holds one\nrun, not the thread."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Both layers assume the work itself is over by the time the client comes back:\nreplaying a log and reading a transcript are both reads of something already\nproduced. A long-running "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"sandboxed agent"}]},{"type":"text","value":" is the case where neither is enough,\nbecause the run is still going and its producer was the host that disappeared. That\nneeds a third thing, a later request that adopts the run and keeps driving it. See\n"},{"type":"link","href":"../sandbox/takeover","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Takeover & Detached Runs"}]},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"why-server-authoritative-is-the-recommended-default","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Why server-authoritative is the recommended default"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"One source of truth."}]},{"type":"text","value":" History lives on the server, so no two copies can drift.\nThe same conversation opens on any device and survives a restart."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A cheap client."}]},{"type":"text","value":" The browser never parses or stores a long transcript, so there\nis no storage quota or startup-parse cost, even for huge threads."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Reload durability anyway."}]},{"type":"text","value":" The mount "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"GET"},{"type":"text","value":" re-paints the transcript and reports\nany "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"activeRun"},{"type":"text","value":", so the client rejoins it and restores pending interrupts."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"No wasted work."}]},{"type":"text","value":" That "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"GET"},{"type":"text","value":" shares the route with the durable-stream resume, and\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"loadThread"},{"type":"text","value":" returns ready-made messages instead of replaying a stream to rebuild\nthem."}]}]}]},{"type":"code","value":"sequenceDiagram\n    participant Hook as useChat (persistence: true)\n    participant Route as GET /api/chat\n    participant Store as Durable store\n    participant Log as Delivery log\n\n    Note over Hook: page reloads while a run is streaming\n    Hook->>Route: ?threadId=support-chat\n    Route->>Store: reconstructChat, loadThread + findActiveRun\n    Store-->>Route: messages + activeRun (runId)\n    Route-->>Hook: transcript + activeRun cursor\n    Note over Hook: transcript paints\n    Hook->>Route: ?runId=…&offset=-1\n    Route->>Log: resumeServerSentEventsResponse\n    Log-->>Hook: replay + live tail of the run\n    Note over Hook: reply finishes in place","lang":"mermaid"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"separate-boundaries","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Separate boundaries"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Server state persistence is one of three boundaries that intentionally share no\ncode:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Server state"}]},{"type":"text","value":" (this page): "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"AIPersistence"},{"type":"text","value":" stores driven by the middleware\nlifecycle, the authoritative record."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Client hydration"}]},{"type":"text","value":": the browser restores a rendered conversation, a separate\nconcern covered in "},{"type":"link","href":"./client-persistence","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Client persistence"}]},{"type":"text","value":"."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Stream delivery"}]},{"type":"text","value":": replaying an in-flight SSE response,\n"},{"type":"link","href":"../resumable-streams/overview","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Resumable Streams"}]},{"type":"text","value":"."}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"State middleware never mutates chunks to add delivery offsets, and it stores\nserver event state, not the client's rendered messages."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"chat-middleware-lifecycle","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Chat middleware lifecycle"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"withPersistence(persistence)"},{"type":"text","value":" derives a plan from store presence:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":true,"start":1,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"setup"},{"type":"text","value":" provides persistence, interrupt, and lock capabilities when their\nstores exist."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"onConfig"},{"type":"text","value":" creates or resumes the run, seeds usage from the existing run\nrecord, loads pending interrupts, and validates the request's resume batch\nagainst them, then merges stored messages into the request by id. An empty\nlist loads the stored thread."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"onUsage"},{"type":"text","value":" accumulates each provider terminal."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"onChunk"},{"type":"text","value":" reacts only to a "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"RUN_FINISHED"},{"type":"text","value":" interrupt outcome. A direct adapter\nterminal arrives before "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"onUsage"},{"type":"text","value":", so the handler includes its usage and\nignores the following "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"onUsage"},{"type":"text","value":". A synthesized tool boundary arrives after\nthe original terminal's "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"onUsage"},{"type":"text","value":", so the handler reuses that aggregate. It\nthen commits accepted resumes, stores the new interrupts, marks the run\ninterrupted, and saves messages."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Before "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"onFinish"},{"type":"text","value":", the chat engine appends the completed terminal assistant\nmessages to "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ctx.messages"},{"type":"text","value":". Native-combined output keeps the structured\nresult on its terminal assistant message. Separate finalization and\nevent-sourced harness output append a second structured-output message only\nwhen that event uses a different message id."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"onFinish"},{"type":"text","value":" saves that canonical transcript before marking the run completed.\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"onError"},{"type":"text","value":" terminalizes the run record without replacing the transcript. Both\nretain known usage. So does terminal "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"onAbort"},{"type":"text","value":", with one exception: on a run\nanother middleware has declared detachable, a plain disconnect (no cancel\nrecorded in either band) writes nothing and leaves the record "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"'running'"},{"type":"text","value":"\nfor a later takeover. See\n"},{"type":"link","href":"../sandbox/takeover#detach-vs-cancel","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Takeover & Detached Runs"}]},{"type":"text","value":"."}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Accepted resumes are committed (interrupts marked resolved/cancelled) only once\nthe run reaches a successful boundary, so a provider failure or abort between\naccepting a resume and reaching that boundary leaves the interrupt pending and a\nretry with the same resume succeeds. The canonical AG-UI chunk stream remains\nunchanged; persistence does not create a second event stream."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"withPersistence"},{"type":"text","value":" merges incoming "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"messages"},{"type":"text","value":" into the stored thread by id.\nIf "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"messages"},{"type":"text","value":" is empty, the stored transcript is loaded and used.\nIf "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"messages"},{"type":"text","value":" is not empty, stored extras stay, new ids append, and the same\nid uses the incoming message. "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"saveThread"},{"type":"text","value":" replaces the stored thread with\nthat merged list."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"reading-the-stores-from-your-own-middleware","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Reading the stores from your own middleware"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Your own middleware often needs the same stores "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"withPersistence"},{"type":"text","value":" is already\nholding: an audit step that writes to "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"metadata"},{"type":"text","value":", a guard that checks pending\ninterrupts. Passing the persistence object in twice works but drifts, because\nthe middleware and your code can end up with different instances."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"withPersistence"},{"type":"text","value":" publishes what it holds as capabilities instead. Declare what\nyou need in "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"requires"},{"type":"text","value":", then read it off the context:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { chat, defineChatMiddleware, toServerSentEventsResponse } from '@tanstack/ai'\nimport { openaiText } from '@tanstack/ai-openai'\nimport {\n  InterruptsCapability,\n  PersistenceCapability,\n  getInterrupts,\n  getPersistence,\n  memoryPersistence,\n  withPersistence,\n} from '@tanstack/ai-persistence'\nimport type { ChatMiddlewareContext } from '@tanstack/ai'\n\nconst persistence = memoryPersistence()\n\nconst auditPending = defineChatMiddleware({\n  name: 'audit-pending',\n  // Fails fast at setup when the capability was never provided.\n  requires: [PersistenceCapability, InterruptsCapability],\n  async setup(ctx: ChatMiddlewareContext) {\n    const stores = getPersistence(ctx).stores\n    const interrupts = getInterrupts(ctx)\n    const pending = await interrupts.listPending(ctx.threadId)\n    await stores.metadata?.set(ctx.threadId, 'pending-count', {\n      count: pending.length,\n    })\n  },\n})\n\nexport async function POST(request: Request) {\n  const { messages, threadId } = await request.json()\n  const stream = chat({\n    adapter: openaiText('gpt-5.5'),\n    messages,\n    threadId,\n    // Order matters: the provider runs before the consumer.\n    middleware: [withPersistence(persistence), auditPending],\n  })\n  return toServerSentEventsResponse(stream)\n}","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Two capabilities are published:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"PersistenceCapability"},{"type":"text","value":", read with "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"getPersistence(ctx)"},{"type":"text","value":": the whole\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"AIPersistence"},{"type":"text","value":" object, so any store it exposes is reachable."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"InterruptsCapability"},{"type":"text","value":", read with "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"getInterrupts(ctx)"},{"type":"text","value":": the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"interrupts"},{"type":"text","value":" store\nalone, published only when persistence actually has one."}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"providePersistence"},{"type":"text","value":" and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"provideInterrupts"},{"type":"text","value":" are the write halves, for a\nmiddleware of your own that supplies the stores instead of "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"withPersistence"},{"type":"text","value":".\nLocks are not part of this: they are a separate capability from\n"},{"type":"link","href":"../advanced/locks","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"@tanstack/ai/locks"}]},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"generation-middleware-lifecycle","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Generation middleware lifecycle"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"withGenerationPersistence(persistence)"},{"type":"text","value":" records the job across\nthree points:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"onStart"},{"type":"text","value":" creates or resumes the run record."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"onFinish"},{"type":"text","value":" / "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"onError"},{"type":"text","value":" / "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"onAbort"},{"type":"text","value":" terminalize it."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A result transform captures the terminal result metadata (ids, urls, never\nmedia bytes) onto the record."}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"When "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"artifacts"},{"type":"text","value":" and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"blobs"},{"type":"text","value":" are both provided it also persists the generated\nmedia and merges the durable refs onto both the result and the run record."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Generation uses its own "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"generationRuns"},{"type":"text","value":" store ("},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"GenerationRunStore"},{"type":"text","value":"), never chat's\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"runs"},{"type":"text","value":" / "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"messages"},{"type":"text","value":". A generation has no conversation, so the run is keyed on\nits own "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"runId"},{"type":"text","value":" ("},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ctx.runId ?? ctx.requestId"},{"type":"text","value":"), and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"threadId"},{"type":"text","value":" never becomes the\njob's primary identity."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"threadId"},{"type":"text","value":" is nonetheless "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"required"}]},{"type":"text","value":": it is the slot the run is filed under,\nand "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"GenerationRunRecord.threadId"},{"type":"text","value":" is a required field. The middleware resolves\nit as "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"opts.threadId ?? ctx.threadId"},{"type":"text","value":" (normally the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"threadId"},{"type":"text","value":" the caller\npassed the activity, with the option as an override) and "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"throws"}]},{"type":"text","value":" when\nneither supplies one. It is never faked from the request id: a run filed under\nan invented scope can never be hydrated by one, so restoring would silently\nreturn nothing forever."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"composition-semantics","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Composition semantics"}]},{"type":"code","value":"import {\n  composePersistence,\n  memoryPersistence,\n} from '@tanstack/ai-persistence'\n\nconst base = memoryPersistence()\nconst replacement = base.stores.messages\n\nconst result = composePersistence(base, {\n  overrides: {\n    messages: replacement,\n    metadata: undefined,\n    interrupts: false,\n  },\n})","lang":"ts"},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"messages"},{"type":"text","value":" is replaced."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"metadata"},{"type":"text","value":" is inherited because the override is "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"undefined"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"interrupts"},{"type":"text","value":" is removed."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"every omitted store is inherited."}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Composition copies the store map and does not mutate or dispose either input.\nThe return type calculates which keys are required, optional, replaced, or\nremoved. Unknown store keys are rejected statically and by runtime validation."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Middleware adds entrypoint validation:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"chat requires "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"messages"},{"type":"text","value":"; rejects "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"interrupts"},{"type":"text","value":" without "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"runs"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"generation requires "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"generationRuns"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"reconstructChat"},{"type":"text","value":" requires "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"messages"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"reconstructGeneration"},{"type":"text","value":" requires "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"generationRuns"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The runtime checks are required because JavaScript, configuration loading, and\nexplicitly widened types can bypass static guarantees."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"backend-ownership","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Backend ownership"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"An adapter owns its own resources: connection lifecycle, when migrations run, and\nhow each store record maps to rows. The middleware only calls the store methods;\nit never opens a connection or inspects a table. A backend may provide any subset\nof the stores (for example, no "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"metadata"},{"type":"text","value":"), and the return type reflects exactly the\nstores it exposes. "},{"type":"link","href":"./build-your-own-chat-adapter","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Build a chat adapter"}]},{"type":"text","value":" shows\nthis end to end for SQLite."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"composePersistence"},{"type":"text","value":" does not add distributed transactions. When related\nstores use different systems, adapter authors must define retry,\nidempotency, and consistency behavior."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Two "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"RunStore"},{"type":"text","value":" details bind an adapter author beyond the obvious mapping, and\nboth exist for durable runs. "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"update"},{"type":"text","value":" must round-trip "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"driverEpoch"},{"type":"text","value":" (the fencing\ntoken a takeover bumps, and the only way a superseded host can discover it lost\nthe run), and it must distinguish an "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"omitted"}]},{"type":"text","value":" patch key (leave the column\nalone) from a key carrying "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"undefined"},{"type":"text","value":" (clear the column). The takeover path\nclears "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"detachedSince"},{"type":"text","value":" exactly that way. See\n"},{"type":"link","href":"./build-your-own-adapter","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Build your own adapter"}]},{"type":"text","value":" and\n"},{"type":"link","href":"../sandbox/takeover#requirements","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Takeover & Detached Runs"}]},{"type":"text","value":"."}]}],"headings":[{"id":"two-layers-not-one","text":"Two layers, not one","level":2},{"id":"identity-threads-and-runs","text":"Identity: threads and runs","level":2},{"id":"isolation-is-yours-to-enforce","text":"Isolation is yours to enforce","level":3},{"id":"who-owns-history-when-both-sides-persist","text":"Who owns history when both sides persist","level":2},{"id":"what-a-reload-restores","text":"What a reload restores","level":2},{"id":"why-server-authoritative-is-the-recommended-default","text":"Why server-authoritative is the recommended default","level":2},{"id":"separate-boundaries","text":"Separate boundaries","level":2},{"id":"chat-middleware-lifecycle","text":"Chat middleware lifecycle","level":2},{"id":"reading-the-stores-from-your-own-middleware","text":"Reading the stores from your own middleware","level":2},{"id":"generation-middleware-lifecycle","text":"Generation middleware lifecycle","level":2},{"id":"composition-semantics","text":"Composition semantics","level":2},{"id":"backend-ownership","text":"Backend ownership","level":2}]},"frameworks":[],"downloadMarkdown":"\nRead this when something surprised you, or before you write a backend. To simply set\npersistence up, [the overview](./overview) is three snippets.\n\n## Two layers, not one\n\n| Layer | Answers | Lives | Docs |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| **Delivery durability** | \"how do I reconnect to a stream that is still running?\" | a per-run log, keyed by `runId` | [Resumable Streams](../resumable-streams/overview) |\n| **State persistence** | \"what is the conversation, and is it still there later?\" | a durable store (client and/or server) | this section |\n\nThey share no code. Delivery durability replays a live byte stream so a dropped\nconnection resumes exactly where it stopped. State persistence stores the\nconversation itself, so it survives a reload or exists on another device. A replayable\nstream is not a saved conversation, and a saved conversation is not a live stream.\nMost real apps want both.\n\n## Identity: threads and runs\n\nA **thread** (`threadId`) is the conversation: stable, survives reloads, exists on\nevery device. A **run** (`runId`) is one execution inside it, minted fresh for each\nstreamed answer. A thread accumulates many runs; delivery durability logs one run,\nstate persistence stores the whole thread.\n\n```mermaid\nflowchart TB\n    subgraph thread [\"One thread, threadId (stable, the conversation)\"]\n        direction LR\n        run1[\"run r1\ncompleted\"] --> run2[\"run r2\ncompleted\"] --> run3[\"run r3\nrunning\"]\n    end\n\n    subgraph delivery [\"Delivery durability, one byte log per run\"]\n        log[\"log for r3\nreplays the live stream to a reconnecting client\"]\n    end\n\n    subgraph state [\"State persistence, durable store per thread\"]\n        store[\"transcript, run records, interrupts\"]\n    end\n\n    run3 -. \"a dropped connection tails\" .-> log\n    thread -- \"saved on finish, loaded on mount\" --> store\n```\n\nRun ids are too ephemeral to reconnect by, since a reloading client may not know the\ncurrent one. Reconnection resolves from the stable `threadId` instead: the store\nanswers \"does this thread have a live run?\" (`findActiveRun`), and only then does the\nclient tail that run's log. [Id map](./id-map) is the short version of both ids.\n\n### Isolation is yours to enforce\n\nStore APIs take a bare `threadId` string, which keeps adapters simple and makes\nmulti-user isolation your job:\n\n- Derive `Scope.userId` / `Scope.tenantId` **server-side** from session state.\n- Authorize before `loadThread` / `saveThread` / `reconstructChat`, with\n  `reconstructChat({ authorize })`.\n- Never treat a client-supplied thread id as ownership. Thread ids are guessable.\n\n`Scope` is re-exported from `@tanstack/ai-persistence`, so the identity type sits next\nto the store contracts.\n\n## Who owns history when both sides persist\n\n`withPersistence` merges incoming `messages` into the stored thread by id:\n\n- **Empty `messages`**: continue from the stored transcript. The server loads\n  its copy and runs from there.\n- **Non-empty `messages`**: merge by id. The last incoming id that already\n  exists in stored is a cutoff. Stored messages after it are dropped. Incoming\n  wins on the same id. New ids are appended.\n\n`saveThread` replaces the stored thread with that merged list.\n\nTwo postures:\n\n- **Client-authoritative**, closest to a pure SPA. The client sends its\n  in-memory list. Merge keeps stored extras the client omitted, unless a\n  shared id cuts the stored tail (reload).\n- **Server-authoritative**: the same thread opens on another device. With\n  `history: { pageSize }`, the client posts only the new turn. Reload posts\n  the last user. Resume posts from that user through the painted assistant.\n  Merge keeps stored extras that sit before the cutoff.\n\n## What a reload restores\n\nWith a client store adapter, `useChat` reads the client record on load and acts on\nwhat it finds:\n\n1. **The run had finished.** The record has the transcript and no resume pointer. The\n   conversation paints instantly from storage, with no network.\n2. **The run was paused on an interrupt.** The resume pointer carries the pending\n   interrupts, so the transcript paints and the approval UI comes back as it was.\n3. **The run was still streaming.** The transcript paints from storage, then the\n   client rejoins the live run through the durability log and the reply finishes in\n   place. This is the one case that needs both layers.\n\nA dropped connection while the page is still open is simpler: delivery durability\nreconnects on its own. Persistence matters once the page itself is gone.\n\nServer-authoritative mode paints from a server read instead of `localStorage`.\nA GET with `limit` returns the newest window and `page`. An older window uses\n`before`. The delivery log cannot supply that history, because it holds one\nrun, not the thread.\n\nBoth layers assume the work itself is over by the time the client comes back:\nreplaying a log and reading a transcript are both reads of something already\nproduced. A long-running **sandboxed agent** is the case where neither is enough,\nbecause the run is still going and its producer was the host that disappeared. That\nneeds a third thing, a later request that adopts the run and keeps driving it. See\n[Takeover & Detached Runs](../sandbox/takeover).\n\n## Why server-authoritative is the recommended default\n\n- **One source of truth.** History lives on the server, so no two copies can drift.\n  The same conversation opens on any device and survives a restart.\n- **A cheap client.** The browser never parses or stores a long transcript, so there\n  is no storage quota or startup-parse cost, even for huge threads.\n- **Reload durability anyway.** The mount `GET` re-paints the transcript and reports\n  any `activeRun`, so the client rejoins it and restores pending interrupts.\n- **No wasted work.** That `GET` shares the route with the durable-stream resume, and\n  `loadThread` returns ready-made messages instead of replaying a stream to rebuild\n  them.\n\n```mermaid\nsequenceDiagram\n    participant Hook as useChat (persistence: true)\n    participant Route as GET /api/chat\n    participant Store as Durable store\n    participant Log as Delivery log\n\n    Note over Hook: page reloads while a run is streaming\n    Hook->>Route: ?threadId=support-chat\n    Route->>Store: reconstructChat, loadThread + findActiveRun\n    Store-->>Route: messages + activeRun (runId)\n    Route-->>Hook: transcript + activeRun cursor\n    Note over Hook: transcript paints\n    Hook->>Route: ?runId=…&offset=-1\n    Route->>Log: resumeServerSentEventsResponse\n    Log-->>Hook: replay + live tail of the run\n    Note over Hook: reply finishes in place\n```\n\n## Separate boundaries\n\nServer state persistence is one of three boundaries that intentionally share no\ncode:\n\n- **Server state** (this page): `AIPersistence` stores driven by the middleware\n  lifecycle, the authoritative record.\n- **Client hydration**: the browser restores a rendered conversation, a separate\n  concern covered in [Client persistence](./client-persistence).\n- **Stream delivery**: replaying an in-flight SSE response,\n  [Resumable Streams](../resumable-streams/overview).\n\nState middleware never mutates chunks to add delivery offsets, and it stores\nserver event state, not the client's rendered messages.\n\n## Chat middleware lifecycle\n\n`withPersistence(persistence)` derives a plan from store presence:\n\n1. `setup` provides persistence, interrupt, and lock capabilities when their\n   stores exist.\n2. `onConfig` creates or resumes the run, seeds usage from the existing run\n   record, loads pending interrupts, and validates the request's resume batch\n   against them, then merges stored messages into the request by id. An empty\n   list loads the stored thread.\n3. `onUsage` accumulates each provider terminal.\n4. `onChunk` reacts only to a `RUN_FINISHED` interrupt outcome. A direct adapter\n   terminal arrives before `onUsage`, so the handler includes its usage and\n   ignores the following `onUsage`. A synthesized tool boundary arrives after\n   the original terminal's `onUsage`, so the handler reuses that aggregate. It\n   then commits accepted resumes, stores the new interrupts, marks the run\n   interrupted, and saves messages.\n5. Before `onFinish`, the chat engine appends the completed terminal assistant\n   messages to `ctx.messages`. Native-combined output keeps the structured\n   result on its terminal assistant message. Separate finalization and\n   event-sourced harness output append a second structured-output message only\n   when that event uses a different message id.\n6. `onFinish` saves that canonical transcript before marking the run completed.\n   `onError` terminalizes the run record without replacing the transcript. Both\n   retain known usage. So does terminal `onAbort`, with one exception: on a run\n   another middleware has declared detachable, a plain disconnect (no cancel\n   recorded in either band) writes nothing and leaves the record `'running'`\n   for a later takeover. See\n   [Takeover & Detached Runs](../sandbox/takeover#detach-vs-cancel).\n\nAccepted resumes are committed (interrupts marked resolved/cancelled) only once\nthe run reaches a successful boundary, so a provider failure or abort between\naccepting a resume and reaching that boundary leaves the interrupt pending and a\nretry with the same resume succeeds. The canonical AG-UI chunk stream remains\nunchanged; persistence does not create a second event stream.\n\n`withPersistence` merges incoming `messages` into the stored thread by id.\nIf `messages` is empty, the stored transcript is loaded and used.\nIf `messages` is not empty, stored extras stay, new ids append, and the same\nid uses the incoming message. `saveThread` replaces the stored thread with\nthat merged list.\n\n## Reading the stores from your own middleware\n\nYour own middleware often needs the same stores `withPersistence` is already\nholding: an audit step that writes to `metadata`, a guard that checks pending\ninterrupts. Passing the persistence object in twice works but drifts, because\nthe middleware and your code can end up with different instances.\n\n`withPersistence` publishes what it holds as capabilities instead. Declare what\nyou need in `requires`, then read it off the context:\n\n```ts\nimport { chat, defineChatMiddleware, toServerSentEventsResponse } from '@tanstack/ai'\nimport { openaiText } from '@tanstack/ai-openai'\nimport {\n  InterruptsCapability,\n  PersistenceCapability,\n  getInterrupts,\n  getPersistence,\n  memoryPersistence,\n  withPersistence,\n} from '@tanstack/ai-persistence'\nimport type { ChatMiddlewareContext } from '@tanstack/ai'\n\nconst persistence = memoryPersistence()\n\nconst auditPending = defineChatMiddleware({\n  name: 'audit-pending',\n  // Fails fast at setup when the capability was never provided.\n  requires: [PersistenceCapability, InterruptsCapability],\n  async setup(ctx: ChatMiddlewareContext) {\n    const stores = getPersistence(ctx).stores\n    const interrupts = getInterrupts(ctx)\n    const pending = await interrupts.listPending(ctx.threadId)\n    await stores.metadata?.set(ctx.threadId, 'pending-count', {\n      count: pending.length,\n    })\n  },\n})\n\nexport async function POST(request: Request) {\n  const { messages, threadId } = await request.json()\n  const stream = chat({\n    adapter: openaiText('gpt-5.5'),\n    messages,\n    threadId,\n    // Order matters: the provider runs before the consumer.\n    middleware: [withPersistence(persistence), auditPending],\n  })\n  return toServerSentEventsResponse(stream)\n}\n```\n\nTwo capabilities are published:\n\n- `PersistenceCapability`, read with `getPersistence(ctx)`: the whole\n  `AIPersistence` object, so any store it exposes is reachable.\n- `InterruptsCapability`, read with `getInterrupts(ctx)`: the `interrupts` store\n  alone, published only when persistence actually has one.\n\n`providePersistence` and `provideInterrupts` are the write halves, for a\nmiddleware of your own that supplies the stores instead of `withPersistence`.\nLocks are not part of this: they are a separate capability from\n[`@tanstack/ai/locks`](../advanced/locks).\n\n## Generation middleware lifecycle\n\n`withGenerationPersistence(persistence)` records the job across\nthree points:\n\n- `onStart` creates or resumes the run record.\n- `onFinish` / `onError` / `onAbort` terminalize it.\n- A result transform captures the terminal result metadata (ids, urls, never\n  media bytes) onto the record.\n\nWhen `artifacts` and `blobs` are both provided it also persists the generated\nmedia and merges the durable refs onto both the result and the run record.\n\nGeneration uses its own `generationRuns` store (`GenerationRunStore`), never chat's\n`runs` / `messages`. A generation has no conversation, so the run is keyed on\nits own `runId` (`ctx.runId ?? ctx.requestId`), and `threadId` never becomes the\njob's primary identity.\n\n`threadId` is nonetheless **required**: it is the slot the run is filed under,\nand `GenerationRunRecord.threadId` is a required field. The middleware resolves\nit as `opts.threadId ?? ctx.threadId` (normally the `threadId` the caller\npassed the activity, with the option as an override) and **throws** when\nneither supplies one. It is never faked from the request id: a run filed under\nan invented scope can never be hydrated by one, so restoring would silently\nreturn nothing forever.\n\n## Composition semantics\n\n```ts\nimport {\n  composePersistence,\n  memoryPersistence,\n} from '@tanstack/ai-persistence'\n\nconst base = memoryPersistence()\nconst replacement = base.stores.messages\n\nconst result = composePersistence(base, {\n  overrides: {\n    messages: replacement,\n    metadata: undefined,\n    interrupts: false,\n  },\n})\n```\n\n- `messages` is replaced.\n- `metadata` is inherited because the override is `undefined`.\n- `interrupts` is removed.\n- every omitted store is inherited.\n\nComposition copies the store map and does not mutate or dispose either input.\nThe return type calculates which keys are required, optional, replaced, or\nremoved. Unknown store keys are rejected statically and by runtime validation.\n\nMiddleware adds entrypoint validation:\n\n- chat requires `messages`; rejects `interrupts` without `runs`.\n- generation requires `generationRuns`.\n- `reconstructChat` requires `messages`.\n- `reconstructGeneration` requires `generationRuns`.\n\nThe runtime checks are required because JavaScript, configuration loading, and\nexplicitly widened types can bypass static guarantees.\n\n## Backend ownership\n\nAn adapter owns its own resources: connection lifecycle, when migrations run, and\nhow each store record maps to rows. The middleware only calls the store methods;\nit never opens a connection or inspects a table. A backend may provide any subset\nof the stores (for example, no `metadata`), and the return type reflects exactly the\nstores it exposes. [Build a chat adapter](./build-your-own-chat-adapter) shows\nthis end to end for SQLite.\n\n`composePersistence` does not add distributed transactions. When related\nstores use different systems, adapter authors must define retry,\nidempotency, and consistency behavior.\n\nTwo `RunStore` details bind an adapter author beyond the obvious mapping, and\nboth exist for durable runs. `update` must round-trip `driverEpoch` (the fencing\ntoken a takeover bumps, and the only way a superseded host can discover it lost\nthe run), and it must distinguish an **omitted** patch key (leave the column\nalone) from a key carrying `undefined` (clear the column). The takeover path\nclears `detachedSince` exactly that way. See\n[Build your own adapter](./build-your-own-adapter) and\n[Takeover & Detached Runs](../sandbox/takeover#requirements).\n","originalRawMarkdown":"---\ntitle: How Persistence Works (Advanced)\nid: internals\ndescription: \"The two durability layers and how they differ, thread and run identity, who owns history when both sides persist, what a reload restores, and the middleware lifecycle behind every backend.\"\nkeywords:\n  - persistence internals\n  - delivery durability vs state persistence\n  - threadId runId\n  - server authoritative\n  - middleware lifecycle\n---\n\nRead this when something surprised you, or before you write a backend. To simply set\npersistence up, [the overview](./overview) is three snippets.\n\n## Two layers, not one\n\n| Layer | Answers | Lives | Docs |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| **Delivery durability** | \"how do I reconnect to a stream that is still running?\" | a per-run log, keyed by `runId` | [Resumable Streams](../resumable-streams/overview) |\n| **State persistence** | \"what is the conversation, and is it still there later?\" | a durable store (client and/or server) | this section |\n\nThey share no code. Delivery durability replays a live byte stream so a dropped\nconnection resumes exactly where it stopped. State persistence stores the\nconversation itself, so it survives a reload or exists on another device. A replayable\nstream is not a saved conversation, and a saved conversation is not a live stream.\nMost real apps want both.\n\n## Identity: threads and runs\n\nA **thread** (`threadId`) is the conversation: stable, survives reloads, exists on\nevery device. A **run** (`runId`) is one execution inside it, minted fresh for each\nstreamed answer. A thread accumulates many runs; delivery durability logs one run,\nstate persistence stores the whole thread.\n\n```mermaid\nflowchart TB\n    subgraph thread [\"One thread, threadId (stable, the conversation)\"]\n        direction LR\n        run1[\"run r1\ncompleted\"] --> run2[\"run r2\ncompleted\"] --> run3[\"run r3\nrunning\"]\n    end\n\n    subgraph delivery [\"Delivery durability, one byte log per run\"]\n        log[\"log for r3\nreplays the live stream to a reconnecting client\"]\n    end\n\n    subgraph state [\"State persistence, durable store per thread\"]\n        store[\"transcript, run records, interrupts\"]\n    end\n\n    run3 -. \"a dropped connection tails\" .-> log\n    thread -- \"saved on finish, loaded on mount\" --> store\n```\n\nRun ids are too ephemeral to reconnect by, since a reloading client may not know the\ncurrent one. Reconnection resolves from the stable `threadId` instead: the store\nanswers \"does this thread have a live run?\" (`findActiveRun`), and only then does the\nclient tail that run's log. [Id map](./id-map) is the short version of both ids.\n\n### Isolation is yours to enforce\n\nStore APIs take a bare `threadId` string, which keeps adapters simple and makes\nmulti-user isolation your job:\n\n- Derive `Scope.userId` / `Scope.tenantId` **server-side** from session state.\n- Authorize before `loadThread` / `saveThread` / `reconstructChat`, with\n  `reconstructChat({ authorize })`.\n- Never treat a client-supplied thread id as ownership. Thread ids are guessable.\n\n`Scope` is re-exported from `@tanstack/ai-persistence`, so the identity type sits next\nto the store contracts.\n\n## Who owns history when both sides persist\n\n`withPersistence` merges incoming `messages` into the stored thread by id:\n\n- **Empty `messages`**: continue from the stored transcript. The server loads\n  its copy and runs from there.\n- **Non-empty `messages`**: merge by id. The last incoming id that already\n  exists in stored is a cutoff. Stored messages after it are dropped. Incoming\n  wins on the same id. New ids are appended.\n\n`saveThread` replaces the stored thread with that merged list.\n\nTwo postures:\n\n- **Client-authoritative**, closest to a pure SPA. The client sends its\n  in-memory list. Merge keeps stored extras the client omitted, unless a\n  shared id cuts the stored tail (reload).\n- **Server-authoritative**: the same thread opens on another device. With\n  `history: { pageSize }`, the client posts only the new turn. Reload posts\n  the last user. Resume posts from that user through the painted assistant.\n  Merge keeps stored extras that sit before the cutoff.\n\n## What a reload restores\n\nWith a client store adapter, `useChat` reads the client record on load and acts on\nwhat it finds:\n\n1. **The run had finished.** The record has the transcript and no resume pointer. The\n   conversation paints instantly from storage, with no network.\n2. **The run was paused on an interrupt.** The resume pointer carries the pending\n   interrupts, so the transcript paints and the approval UI comes back as it was.\n3. **The run was still streaming.** The transcript paints from storage, then the\n   client rejoins the live run through the durability log and the reply finishes in\n   place. This is the one case that needs both layers.\n\nA dropped connection while the page is still open is simpler: delivery durability\nreconnects on its own. Persistence matters once the page itself is gone.\n\nServer-authoritative mode paints from a server read instead of `localStorage`.\nA GET with `limit` returns the newest window and `page`. An older window uses\n`before`. The delivery log cannot supply that history, because it holds one\nrun, not the thread.\n\nBoth layers assume the work itself is over by the time the client comes back:\nreplaying a log and reading a transcript are both reads of something already\nproduced. A long-running **sandboxed agent** is the case where neither is enough,\nbecause the run is still going and its producer was the host that disappeared. That\nneeds a third thing, a later request that adopts the run and keeps driving it. See\n[Takeover & Detached Runs](../sandbox/takeover).\n\n## Why server-authoritative is the recommended default\n\n- **One source of truth.** History lives on the server, so no two copies can drift.\n  The same conversation opens on any device and survives a restart.\n- **A cheap client.** The browser never parses or stores a long transcript, so there\n  is no storage quota or startup-parse cost, even for huge threads.\n- **Reload durability anyway.** The mount `GET` re-paints the transcript and reports\n  any `activeRun`, so the client rejoins it and restores pending interrupts.\n- **No wasted work.** That `GET` shares the route with the durable-stream resume, and\n  `loadThread` returns ready-made messages instead of replaying a stream to rebuild\n  them.\n\n```mermaid\nsequenceDiagram\n    participant Hook as useChat (persistence: true)\n    participant Route as GET /api/chat\n    participant Store as Durable store\n    participant Log as Delivery log\n\n    Note over Hook: page reloads while a run is streaming\n    Hook->>Route: ?threadId=support-chat\n    Route->>Store: reconstructChat, loadThread + findActiveRun\n    Store-->>Route: messages + activeRun (runId)\n    Route-->>Hook: transcript + activeRun cursor\n    Note over Hook: transcript paints\n    Hook->>Route: ?runId=…&offset=-1\n    Route->>Log: resumeServerSentEventsResponse\n    Log-->>Hook: replay + live tail of the run\n    Note over Hook: reply finishes in place\n```\n\n## Separate boundaries\n\nServer state persistence is one of three boundaries that intentionally share no\ncode:\n\n- **Server state** (this page): `AIPersistence` stores driven by the middleware\n  lifecycle, the authoritative record.\n- **Client hydration**: the browser restores a rendered conversation, a separate\n  concern covered in [Client persistence](./client-persistence).\n- **Stream delivery**: replaying an in-flight SSE response,\n  [Resumable Streams](../resumable-streams/overview).\n\nState middleware never mutates chunks to add delivery offsets, and it stores\nserver event state, not the client's rendered messages.\n\n## Chat middleware lifecycle\n\n`withPersistence(persistence)` derives a plan from store presence:\n\n1. `setup` provides persistence, interrupt, and lock capabilities when their\n   stores exist.\n2. `onConfig` creates or resumes the run, seeds usage from the existing run\n   record, loads pending interrupts, and validates the request's resume batch\n   against them, then merges stored messages into the request by id. An empty\n   list loads the stored thread.\n3. `onUsage` accumulates each provider terminal.\n4. `onChunk` reacts only to a `RUN_FINISHED` interrupt outcome. A direct adapter\n   terminal arrives before `onUsage`, so the handler includes its usage and\n   ignores the following `onUsage`. A synthesized tool boundary arrives after\n   the original terminal's `onUsage`, so the handler reuses that aggregate. It\n   then commits accepted resumes, stores the new interrupts, marks the run\n   interrupted, and saves messages.\n5. Before `onFinish`, the chat engine appends the completed terminal assistant\n   messages to `ctx.messages`. Native-combined output keeps the structured\n   result on its terminal assistant message. Separate finalization and\n   event-sourced harness output append a second structured-output message only\n   when that event uses a different message id.\n6. `onFinish` saves that canonical transcript before marking the run completed.\n   `onError` terminalizes the run record without replacing the transcript. Both\n   retain known usage. So does terminal `onAbort`, with one exception: on a run\n   another middleware has declared detachable, a plain disconnect (no cancel\n   recorded in either band) writes nothing and leaves the record `'running'`\n   for a later takeover. See\n   [Takeover & Detached Runs](../sandbox/takeover#detach-vs-cancel).\n\nAccepted resumes are committed (interrupts marked resolved/cancelled) only once\nthe run reaches a successful boundary, so a provider failure or abort between\naccepting a resume and reaching that boundary leaves the interrupt pending and a\nretry with the same resume succeeds. The canonical AG-UI chunk stream remains\nunchanged; persistence does not create a second event stream.\n\n`withPersistence` merges incoming `messages` into the stored thread by id.\nIf `messages` is empty, the stored transcript is loaded and used.\nIf `messages` is not empty, stored extras stay, new ids append, and the same\nid uses the incoming message. `saveThread` replaces the stored thread with\nthat merged list.\n\n## Reading the stores from your own middleware\n\nYour own middleware often needs the same stores `withPersistence` is already\nholding: an audit step that writes to `metadata`, a guard that checks pending\ninterrupts. Passing the persistence object in twice works but drifts, because\nthe middleware and your code can end up with different instances.\n\n`withPersistence` publishes what it holds as capabilities instead. Declare what\nyou need in `requires`, then read it off the context:\n\n```ts\nimport { chat, defineChatMiddleware, toServerSentEventsResponse } from '@tanstack/ai'\nimport { openaiText } from '@tanstack/ai-openai'\nimport {\n  InterruptsCapability,\n  PersistenceCapability,\n  getInterrupts,\n  getPersistence,\n  memoryPersistence,\n  withPersistence,\n} from '@tanstack/ai-persistence'\nimport type { ChatMiddlewareContext } from '@tanstack/ai'\n\nconst persistence = memoryPersistence()\n\nconst auditPending = defineChatMiddleware({\n  name: 'audit-pending',\n  // Fails fast at setup when the capability was never provided.\n  requires: [PersistenceCapability, InterruptsCapability],\n  async setup(ctx: ChatMiddlewareContext) {\n    const stores = getPersistence(ctx).stores\n    const interrupts = getInterrupts(ctx)\n    const pending = await interrupts.listPending(ctx.threadId)\n    await stores.metadata?.set(ctx.threadId, 'pending-count', {\n      count: pending.length,\n    })\n  },\n})\n\nexport async function POST(request: Request) {\n  const { messages, threadId } = await request.json()\n  const stream = chat({\n    adapter: openaiText('gpt-5.5'),\n    messages,\n    threadId,\n    // Order matters: the provider runs before the consumer.\n    middleware: [withPersistence(persistence), auditPending],\n  })\n  return toServerSentEventsResponse(stream)\n}\n```\n\nTwo capabilities are published:\n\n- `PersistenceCapability`, read with `getPersistence(ctx)`: the whole\n  `AIPersistence` object, so any store it exposes is reachable.\n- `InterruptsCapability`, read with `getInterrupts(ctx)`: the `interrupts` store\n  alone, published only when persistence actually has one.\n\n`providePersistence` and `provideInterrupts` are the write halves, for a\nmiddleware of your own that supplies the stores instead of `withPersistence`.\nLocks are not part of this: they are a separate capability from\n[`@tanstack/ai/locks`](../advanced/locks).\n\n## Generation middleware lifecycle\n\n`withGenerationPersistence(persistence)` records the job across\nthree points:\n\n- `onStart` creates or resumes the run record.\n- `onFinish` / `onError` / `onAbort` terminalize it.\n- A result transform captures the terminal result metadata (ids, urls, never\n  media bytes) onto the record.\n\nWhen `artifacts` and `blobs` are both provided it also persists the generated\nmedia and merges the durable refs onto both the result and the run record.\n\nGeneration uses its own `generationRuns` store (`GenerationRunStore`), never chat's\n`runs` / `messages`. A generation has no conversation, so the run is keyed on\nits own `runId` (`ctx.runId ?? ctx.requestId`), and `threadId` never becomes the\njob's primary identity.\n\n`threadId` is nonetheless **required**: it is the slot the run is filed under,\nand `GenerationRunRecord.threadId` is a required field. The middleware resolves\nit as `opts.threadId ?? ctx.threadId` (normally the `threadId` the caller\npassed the activity, with the option as an override) and **throws** when\nneither supplies one. It is never faked from the request id: a run filed under\nan invented scope can never be hydrated by one, so restoring would silently\nreturn nothing forever.\n\n## Composition semantics\n\n```ts\nimport {\n  composePersistence,\n  memoryPersistence,\n} from '@tanstack/ai-persistence'\n\nconst base = memoryPersistence()\nconst replacement = base.stores.messages\n\nconst result = composePersistence(base, {\n  overrides: {\n    messages: replacement,\n    metadata: undefined,\n    interrupts: false,\n  },\n})\n```\n\n- `messages` is replaced.\n- `metadata` is inherited because the override is `undefined`.\n- `interrupts` is removed.\n- every omitted store is inherited.\n\nComposition copies the store map and does not mutate or dispose either input.\nThe return type calculates which keys are required, optional, replaced, or\nremoved. Unknown store keys are rejected statically and by runtime validation.\n\nMiddleware adds entrypoint validation:\n\n- chat requires `messages`; rejects `interrupts` without `runs`.\n- generation requires `generationRuns`.\n- `reconstructChat` requires `messages`.\n- `reconstructGeneration` requires `generationRuns`.\n\nThe runtime checks are required because JavaScript, configuration loading, and\nexplicitly widened types can bypass static guarantees.\n\n## Backend ownership\n\nAn adapter owns its own resources: connection lifecycle, when migrations run, and\nhow each store record maps to rows. The middleware only calls the store methods;\nit never opens a connection or inspects a table. A backend may provide any subset\nof the stores (for example, no `metadata`), and the return type reflects exactly the\nstores it exposes. [Build a chat adapter](./build-your-own-chat-adapter) shows\nthis end to end for SQLite.\n\n`composePersistence` does not add distributed transactions. When related\nstores use different systems, adapter authors must define retry,\nidempotency, and consistency behavior.\n\nTwo `RunStore` details bind an adapter author beyond the obvious mapping, and\nboth exist for durable runs. `update` must round-trip `driverEpoch` (the fencing\ntoken a takeover bumps, and the only way a superseded host can discover it lost\nthe run), and it must distinguish an **omitted** patch key (leave the column\nalone) from a key carrying `undefined` (clear the column). The takeover path\nclears `detachedSince` exactly that way. See\n[Build your own adapter](./build-your-own-adapter) and\n[Takeover & Detached Runs](../sandbox/takeover#requirements).\n"}
